window.docContents[85].resolve({"/Attributes/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Attributes--Attributes-as-Modifiers":{"contents":"Attributes can be added to declarations as a declaration modifier.\nThey are placed between the documentation comment and the visibility modifiers.\n\nAttributes\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Attributes","header":"9.1. Attributes as Modifiers","id":"/Attributes/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Attributes--Attributes-as-Modifiers"},"/Type-Classes/Instance-Declarations/#recursive-instances":{"contents":"Functions defined in where structure definition syntax are not recursive.\nBecause instance declaration is a version of structure definition, type class methods are also not recursive by default.\nInstances for recursive inductive types are common, however.\nThere is a standard idiom to work around this limitation: define a recursive function independently of the instance, and then refer to it in the instance definition.\nBy convention, these recursive functions have the name of the corresponding method, but are defined in the type's namespace.\n\nInstances are not recursiveGiven this definition of NatTree:inductive NatTree where\n  | leaf\n  | branch (left : NatTree) (val : Nat) (right : NatTree)\nthe following BEq instance fails:instance : BEq NatTree where\n  beq\n    | .leaf, .leaf =>\n      true\n    | .branch l1 v1 r1, .branch l2 v2 r2 =>\n      l1 == l2 && v1 == v2 && r1 == r2\n    | _, _ =>\n      false\nwith errors in both the left and right recursive calls that read:failed to synthesize instance of type class\n  BEq NatTree\n\nHint: Adding the command `deriving instance BEq for NatTree` may allow Lean to derive the missing instance.\nGiven a suitable recursive function, such as NatTree.beq:def NatTree.beq : NatTree → NatTree → Bool\n  | .leaf, .leaf =>\n    true\n  | .branch l1 v1 r1, .branch l2 v2 r2 =>\n    NatTree.beq l1 l2 && v1 == v2 && NatTree.beq r1 r2\n  | _, _ =>\n    false\nthe instance can be created in a second step:instance : BEq NatTree where\n  beq := NatTree.beq\nor, equivalently, using anonymous constructor syntax:instance : BEq NatTree := ⟨NatTree.beq⟩\n\n\nFurthermore, instances are not available for instance synthesis during their own definitions.\nThey are first marked as being available for instance synthesis after they are defined.\nNested inductive types, in which the recursive occurrence of the type occurs as a parameter to some other inductive type, may require an instance to be available to write even the recursive function.\nThe standard idiom to work around this limitation is to create a local instance in a recursively-defined function that includes a reference to the function being defined, taking advantage of the fact that instance synthesis may use every binding in the local context with the right type.\n\nInstances for nested typesIn this definition of NatRoseTree, the type being defined occurs nested under another inductive type constructor (Array):inductive NatRoseTree where\n  | node (val : Nat) (children : Array NatRoseTree)\n\nChecking the equality of rose trees requires checking equality of arrays.\nHowever, instances are not typically available for instance synthesis during their own definitions, so the following definition fails, even though NatRoseTree.beq is a recursive function and is in scope in its own definition.def NatRoseTree.beq : (tree1 tree2 : NatRoseTree) → Bool\n  | .node val1 children1, .node val2 children2 =>\n    val1 == val2 &&\n    children1 == children2\nfailed to synthesize instance of type class\n  BEq (Array NatRoseTree)\n\nHint: Type class instance resolution failures can be inspected with the `set_option trace.Meta.synthInstance true` command.\nTo solve this, a local BEq NatRoseTree instance may be let-bound:partial def NatRoseTree.beq : (tree1 tree2 : NatRoseTree) → Bool\n  | .node val1 children1, .node val2 children2 =>\n    let _ : BEq NatRoseTree := ⟨NatRoseTree.beq⟩\n    val1 == val2 &&\n    children1 == children2\nThe use of array equality on the children finds the let-bound instance during instance synthesis.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Type Classes\u0009Instance Declarations","header":"10.2.1. Recursive Instances","id":"/Type-Classes/Instance-Declarations/#recursive-instances"},"/ValidatingProofs/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Validating-a-Lean-Proof--Printing-Axioms--Instructions":{"contents":"Write #print axioms thmName after the theorem declaration, with thmName replaced by the name of the theorem and check that it reports only the built-in axioms propext, Classical.choice, and Quot.sound.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Validating a Lean Proof\u0009Printing Axioms","header":"Instructions","id":"/ValidatingProofs/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Validating-a-Lean-Proof--Printing-Axioms--Instructions"},"/releases/v4.25.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___25___0-_LPAR_2025-11-14_RPAR_--Highlights--InfoView-Trace-Search":{"contents":"#10365 implements the server-side for a new trace search mechanism in\nthe InfoView. See the PR description for a demo video.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.25.0 (2025-11-14)\u0009Highlights","header":"InfoView Trace Search","id":"/releases/v4.25.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___25___0-_LPAR_2025-11-14_RPAR_--Highlights--InfoView-Trace-Search"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Language":{"contents":"* #15839\nfixes a race condition that was caused by incorrect code that was supposed to persist scoped extension state between module docstrings, but failed to do so. The issue is fixed by removing the incorrectly-implemented feature, which may be replaced by a correct version in a future PR.* #15572\nadds a hint/code action the the \"[deprecated]` attribute should specify the date or library version at which the deprecation was introduced\" warning.* #15499\nmakes the statements of generated equation lemmas (f.eq_1, f.eq_2, …) independent of the options where the equations are first requested. A local set_option on the first proof that used the equations could change their statements. Proofs elaborate in parallel, so the statements could differ between builds of the same file, and a proof could compile with some thread counts and fail with others.* #15485\nskips running non-stateful linters if the command has a syntax error and showPartialSyntaxErrors is disabled.* #14316\nmakes the type class resolution cache persist across commands: a result whose query contains no metavariables, local instances or free variables and which is itself closed is kept for later commands, so identical queries there are served from the cache instead of being searched again. Such an entry is only served while the dependencies it recorded are unchanged, so new instances, unification hints and reducibility changes invalidate exactly the affected entries. On the build of the standard library this reduces type class resolution time by about 14% and total instructions by 1.2%; Mathlib gains 0.2% overall, as most of its queries involve local instances, which are not (yet) persisted.* #15399\nfixes the type class resolution cache serving a result after a declaration it could have observed changed, most notably through a later attribute [reducible] or a scoped or local reducibility attribute coming into or going out of effect. As the cache currently lives within a single command, this is rarely visible yet, but it is a necessary prerequisite for sharing the cache across commands, where such changes are routine.* #15319\nadds Expr.hasAnyMVar and Expr.containsMVar functions analogous to the existing Expr.hasAnyFVar and Expr.containsFVar functions.* #15355\nmakes SavedState.restore drop the type class resolution cache entries that were recorded after a tracked environment change it rolls back. The generations validating an entry roll back with the environment while Meta.Cache survives the restore, so a different change made afterwards could bring the recorded generations back and let a stale entry be served; for example, an instance added and rolled back in a failed attempt could still be returned after a different instance was added. Apart from closing such edge cases, this is a necessary prerequisite for cross-command caching, whose entries must survive backtracking without being revalidated against a rolled-back state.* #15354\nmakes a type class resolution cache entry record the state of the instances and unification hints its search consulted, so that the entry is no longer served once they change, even through environment changes that do not clear the cache. For example, an instance added by a command run through liftCommandElabM is now found by later queries in the same computation, and so are scoped instances and unification hints activated at CoreM level. Apart from closing such edge cases, this is a necessary prerequisite for cross-command caching.* #15325\nmakes type class resolution cache results depend on the options the search read. Previously a cached result was reused regardless of options within a command, so a result obtained under a term-level set_option, for example of synthInstance.maxSize, could be served where the option had a different value. Options the search never reads still do not partition the cache. Apart from fixing such in-command edge cases, this is a prerequisite to caching across commands.* #15313\nmakes realizing the equation lemmas of large mutual partial_fixpoint blocks much faster (about 13× for a block of 200 functions), by no longer re-deriving and re-checking the fixpoint equation of the whole block for every function.* #11386\nadds theorems tagged with wf_preprocess for making it possible to conveniently write functions over nested inductive types such as trees that use List.any, in the same way that they're currently able to use List.map and other common list functions.* #15286\nmakes a tactic marked by deprecated_syntax warn no more than once per call. The tactics that its expansion uses no longer warn again. For example, if mintro is deprecated, mintro a b c expands to mintro a; mintro b c and printed five warnings; now it prints one.* #15216\nmakes inferInstanceAs reuse the existing instances of a non-exposed definition. This is done by checking the reusability of an instance in the private scope, as neither the auxiliary definitions being generated nor the ones in the existing instance are exposed in this case.* #15204\nrejects the ← simp-argument modifier when the argument is a simproc or a simp extension, instead of silently ignoring it.* #15237\nadds Environment.realizationEnvContains forConst c, which reports whether realizeConst forConst callbacks can access c. This is the criterion for choosing forConst when a realization depends on multiple constants: the target must be one whose realization environment contains all others.* #14537\nimproves on the error messages of rfl and a few other tactics that fail due to a failed definitional-equality check. There was already a mechanism that analyzed what parts of the term need to be shown explicitly so that the mismatch becomes visible, but it sometimes ran with the wrong configuration. This change fixes these problems.* #15242\nspeeds up the elaboration of structurally recursive definitions by removing work from the construction of the brecOn application that has no effect on the result.* #15240\nmakes Meta.isProofQuick return a definite answer for applications such as match auxiliary functions, casesOn, brecOn, and recursors, whose type is a motive variable applied to arguments. Previously these fell through to isProof's slow path, which infers the type and reduces it to a sort.* #15239\nremoves a redundant abstractNestedProofs pass when elaborating structurally recursive definitions, which reduces elaboration time for files with many such definitions.* #15238\nremoves the per-declaration overhead #15232 introduced in the LCNF declaration extensions, which cost about 1% of instructions when compiling modules with many declarations, while keeping the linear-time replay of realized constants.* #15232\nmakes the cost of realizing a constant (e.g. the equation lemmas needed by simp only [f]) independent of the size of the current file. Previously, files with many such realizations slowed down quadratically: on a file with 4,000 definition/theorem pairs elaboration time drops from 99 s to 32 s, and the time per pair is now constant.* #15230\nspeeds up the elaboration of definitions using match, and of other auxiliary definitions created with mkAuxDefinition and mkAuxTheorem with zetaDelta := false. On a file with 12,800 small pattern-matching definitions, cumulative elaboration time drops from about 19s to about 17s.* #15228\nfixes a per-command overhead in the frontend that grew linearly with the position of the command in the file, making files with many commands quadratic.* #15223\nfixes a quadratic slowdown when elaborating files with many recursive definitions. The share common exprs step of definition elaboration hashed the whole input file once per recursive call, so a file with thousands of structurally or well-founded recursive definitions could spend most of its elaboration time there.* #15091\nadds throws e => R clauses to def contracts, after ensures, stating the exception postcondition of the generated specification theorem. A clause binds the raised exception and, in a state monad, the state at the throw. An ascription throws (e : ε) s => R selects the exception layer of type ε, and an unascribed binder selects the outermost layer. Every unmentioned exception layer keeps the postcondition ⊥, so the specification still rules out its exceptions, while an omitted ensures keeps the postcondition ⊤.* #15064\nreplaces the parser used for Verso docstrings with one that correctly tracks all details of the underlying concrete syntax. Along the way, the API for docstring extensions was improved and parser error messages were made much more actionable. This unblocks automatically formatting Verso docstring syntax.* #15199\nremoves the internal.parseQuotWithCurrentStage options from Lean.Elab.BuiltinDo.Let and Lean.Elab.Do.InferControlInfo that were introduced for bootstrapping reasons, recovering the build-instruction regression that #15090 introduced.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0 (2026-11-16)","header":"Language","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Language"}});